/* Knowledge column (English, from zh-Hans master) */

"kb.origins.title" = "What Is the Zhouyi?";
"kb.origins.sub" = "Three Yi, text and commentary, three meanings";
"kb.origins.body" = "## The Three Yi\nAccording to the Rites of Zhou (Zhouli), in its “Offices of Spring,” the Grand Diviner of antiquity oversaw “the methods of the three Yi”: the Lianshan (Linked Mountains), the Guicang (Return to the Hidden) and the Zhouyi. Tradition holds that the Lianshan belonged to the Xia dynasty and opened with Keeping Still (Gen), while the Guicang belonged to the Shang and opened with The Receptive (Kun). The Rites of Zhou also says of the three that “their basic trigrams all number eight, and their doubled figures sixty-four,” so all three were built on the eight trigrams and sixty-four hexagrams, differing only in arrangement and interpretation. The first two books were lost long ago; the Zhouyi is the only one we can still read in full. As for the “Zhou” in its title, some take it to mean the Zhou dynasty, others read it as “all-encompassing” and “ever-circulating.”\n\n## Classic and Commentaries\nThe Zhouyi has two layers: the classic and the commentaries. The classic consists of the figures and Judgments of the sixty-four hexagrams and the texts of their 384 lines, probably composed during the Western Zhou. The commentaries are ten texts that explain the classic, known together as the Ten Wings; most scholars date them from the Warring States period to the early Han. The classic is terse and archaic and was originally used for divination; the commentaries turned its images and Judgments toward philosophy, transforming the Zhouyi from a diviner's manual into the first of the Confucian classics. In recent decades, excavated texts such as the silk-manuscript Zhouyi from the Han tombs at Mawangdui and the Chu bamboo-slip Zhouyi held by the Shanghai Museum have come to light, letting us see versions older than the received text.\n\n## Three Sages\nThe Book of Han's “Treatise on Literature” says the Changes “passed through three sages across three ages of antiquity”: Fu Xi looked up at the patterns of the heavens and down at the forms of the earth and drew the eight trigrams; King Wen doubled them into the sixty-four hexagrams and wrote the Judgments (some say the line texts came from the Duke of Zhou); and Confucius wrote the Ten Wings to unfold their meaning. This is the traditional account, handed down for two thousand years. Most modern scholars instead see the Zhouyi as the work of many generations, gradually gathered and edited, rather than the creation of one person at one time.\n\n## Three Meanings of Yi\nIn the Han dynasty, both the apocryphal Qianzaodu and the scholar Zheng Xuan taught that “the one name Yi holds three meanings”: simplicity, change and constancy. Simplicity: the way of heaven and earth is plain and easy at heart, something we can know and follow. Change: all things are always changing, and no situation stays fixed. Constancy: within change there are lasting patterns. The Eastern Han dictionary Shuowen Jiezi by Xu Shen also cites the saying that the character for Yi is made of sun and moon: the sun stands for yang, the moon for yin, and their alternation shows yin and yang in endless return. To read the Zhouyi is to learn to see what stays constant within change, and to find simplicity again amid complexity.";

"kb.yinyang.title" = "Yin, Yang and the Lines";
"kb.yinyang.sub" = "Firm and yielding, the source of all things";
"kb.yinyang.body" = "## Two Basic Symbols\nEvery figure in the Zhouyi is built from just two kinds of stroke: an unbroken line “—” is a yang line, and a line broken in the middle “--” is a yin line. The word for line, yao, originally suggests crossing and interweaving. The Great Commentary (Xici) says, “The lines imitate the movements of all under heaven”: each line mirrors the changes of the world.\n\n## Firm and Yielding\nThe yang line stands for the firm and strong, the active, the expanding and the bright; the yin line for the yielding and receptive, the gathering-in and the quiet. Heaven and earth, day and night, movement and stillness, advance and retreat can all be summed up by this one pair. Keep in mind that yin and yang do not mean good and bad: firmness pushed too far easily snaps, while yielding at the right moment can get things done. What the Zhouyi values is firm and yielding balancing each other, each where it belongs. Among the sixty-four hexagrams, only The Creative (Qian) has six yang lines and only The Receptive (Kun) six yin lines; the other sixty-two mix yin and yang, just as real life rarely offers a situation of one pure kind.\n\n## Mutual Roots, Ebb and Flow\nYin and yang are not two separate things. There is yang within yin and yin within yang, and neither can exist without the other: this is called their mutual rootedness. As one wanes the other waxes, and whatever peaks begins to decline, like the cold and heat of the turning seasons: this is their ebb and flow. The Great Commentary sums it up: “One yin, one yang: this is called the Way,” and also, “The firm and the yielding displace each other, and change arises within them.” The world runs on the alternation and cooperation of these two forces. Each arrangement of yin and yang lines in the sixty-four hexagrams captures one moment of this ebb and flow.\n\n## Why Nine and Six\nIn the line texts you will meet names like “Nine at the beginning” and “Six in the second place”: yang lines are called nine and yin lines six. One common explanation comes from yarrow-stalk divination: each line ends up as one of the numbers six, seven, eight or nine; nine is Old Yang and six is Old Yin, and these are the ones that change. Since the Zhouyi is about watching change, nine and six became the names for yang and yin lines. Another explanation looks at the numbers themselves: the yang numbers one, three and five add up to nine, and the yin numbers two and four add up to six. Pairing these names with each line's position gives the traditional line names, from “Nine at the beginning” and “Nine in the second place” to “Six at the top.”\n\n## In This App\nIn this app's hexagram figures, a yang line is drawn as one solid bar and a yin line as two segments with a gap in the middle. The six lines are stacked from the bottom up, and the lowest is Line 1. Changing lines are marked in the accent color, a reminder that this is where change is happening.";

"kb.bagua.title" = "The Eight Trigrams";
"kb.bagua.sub" = "Heaven, Lake, Fire, Thunder, Wind, Water, Mountain, Earth";
"kb.bagua.body" = "## Three Lines Make a Trigram\nStacking three yin or yang lines gives eight possible combinations: the eight trigrams. Of the three lines, the bottom represents earth, the middle humanity and the top heaven, together called the Three Powers. Tradition says Fu Xi drew the trigrams, and the Great Commentary says they serve “to communicate with the virtue of the spirit-like and to classify the nature of all things.” Each trigram has its own natural image and character:\nHeaven ☰ (Qian): strong and tireless; Lake ☱ (Dui): joyful;\nFire ☲ (Li): clinging and bright; Thunder ☳ (Zhen): arousing;\nWind ☴ (Xun): gentle and penetrating; Water ☵ (Kan): perilous;\nMountain ☶ (Gen): still; Earth ☷ (Kun): yielding and devoted.\nThunder, Water and Mountain have one yang line and two yin lines and are called yang trigrams; Wind, Fire and Lake have one yin line and two yang lines and are called yin trigrams. As the Great Commentary puts it, “Yang trigrams have more yin lines; yin trigrams have more yang lines.”\n\n## Images and the Five Phases\nThe Discussion of the Trigrams (Shuogua) also likens the trigrams to a family: Heaven is the father and Earth the mother; Thunder, Water and Mountain are the eldest, middle and youngest sons; Wind, Fire and Lake the eldest, middle and youngest daughters. They are further matched with parts of the body, animals, directions and seasons (Heaven is the horse and the head, Earth the ox and the belly), forming a vast system of images that can spark associations as you read a hexagram. Later tradition also paired them with the Five Phases: Heaven and Lake belong to Metal, Fire to Fire, Thunder and Wind to Wood, Water to Water, and Mountain and Earth to Earth.\n\n## The Early Heaven Numbers\nThe Song-dynasty school of Shao Yong numbers the trigrams Heaven 1, Lake 2, Fire 3, Thunder 4, Wind 5, Water 6, Mountain 7, Earth 8; these are called the Early Heaven numbers. The order comes from the Great Commentary: “In the Changes there is the Supreme Ultimate, which gives rise to the Two Modes; the Two Modes give rise to the Four Images; the Four Images give rise to the Eight Trigrams.” By dividing each level in two, Shao Yong arrived at exactly Heaven 1 through Earth 8. This app's Number cast and Time cast both use these numbers: whatever the remainder is, the matching trigram is taken. For example, a remainder of 3 gives Fire, and a remainder of 0 counts as 8, giving Earth.\n\n## Early and Later Heaven Arrangements\nThe trigrams also have two famous directional diagrams. The Early Heaven arrangement, attributed to Fu Xi, places Heaven in the south and Earth in the north, Fire in the east and Water in the west; it expresses heaven and earth facing each other, yin and yang as complementary opposites. The Later Heaven arrangement, attributed to King Wen, places Fire in the south and Water in the north, Thunder in the east and Lake in the west; it expresses the turning of the seasons and the growth of all things, and later generations mostly used it for directions and seasonal timing. Both diagrams became widely known only after the Song dynasty. This app uses only the Early Heaven numbers for casting, not the directions.";

"kb.sixtyfour.title" = "The 64 Hexagrams";
"kb.sixtyfour.sub" = "Trigrams doubled into 64 situations";
"kb.sixtyfour.body" = "## Doubling the Trigrams\nStack one trigram on top of another and you get a six-line doubled figure, a hexagram; eight times eight makes sixty-four. Each hexagram has a Judgment and each of its six lines a line text, 384 lines in all; The Creative and The Receptive each have an extra text, “All Nines” and “All Sixes,” so some count 386. The bottom three lines form the lower trigram, also called the inner trigram; the top three form the upper trigram, also called the outer trigram. The inner trigram tends to concern yourself and the starting point; the outer, your surroundings and where things are heading. Hexagram names often echo the images of their two trigrams: Water above Heaven, vapor in the sky waiting to fall as rain, is named Waiting (Xu); Fire above Water, the two not meeting, is named Before Completion (Weiji). When this app shows “Hexagram N · X over Y” in a reading, that is the hexagram's number in the received text and its upper and lower trigrams.\n\n## Upper and Lower Canon\nThe received Zhouyi is divided into two parts: the Upper Canon has thirty hexagrams, from The Creative and The Receptive to The Abysmal and The Clinging; the Lower Canon has thirty-four, from Influence and Duration to After Completion and Before Completion. An old view holds that the Upper Canon speaks of the way of heaven and the Lower of human affairs: Influence is about mutual attraction and Duration about lasting, and together they were seen as the beginning of marriage and human relationships. The Upper Canon ends with The Abysmal and The Clinging, the Lower with After Completion and Before Completion; all four are made of Water and Fire, so the two halves echo each other.\n\n## The King Wen Sequence\nThis order is traditionally credited to King Wen, hence the name King Wen sequence. The Tang scholar Kong Yingda described its pattern as “paired two by two: if not inverted, then transformed.” Each pair of neighboring hexagrams is either turned upside down, like Initial Difficulty and Youthful Folly, or has all six lines reversed, like The Creative and The Receptive, or The Abysmal and The Clinging. The Sequence of the Hexagrams (Xugua), one of the Ten Wings, tries to explain the logic: “Only after heaven and earth exist are the myriad things born,” so The Creative and The Receptive are followed by Initial Difficulty, the hardship of first birth; what is newly born is necessarily immature, so next comes Youthful Folly… One by one, the hexagrams string together a story of things sprouting, growing, flourishing, declining and transforming.\n\n## Meaning at Both Ends\nThe book opens with The Creative and The Receptive, heaven and earth set in place, the source of all things. It closes with After Completion and Before Completion: in After Completion all six lines stand in their proper places, a sign that the task is done, yet right after it comes Before Completion, the task not yet finished. The Sequence of the Hexagrams says, “Things can never come to an end; therefore Before Completion follows, and with it the book ends.” Placing “not yet complete” last shows that, for the Zhouyi, the world has no true end point: every ending is a new beginning.";

"kb.yao.title" = "Line Positions";
"kb.yao.sub" = "Bottom to top, each place has its time";
"kb.yao.body" = "## From the Bottom Up\nA hexagram's six lines are counted from the bottom: first, second, third, fourth, fifth and top. Moving upward, they trace a process from sprouting to maturity to the very end: the first line is the beginning, still hidden; the top line is the limit, where things often turn into their opposite. A line's name combines yin or yang with its position: a yang line in the first place is “Nine at the beginning,” a yin line in the second place “Six in the second place,” and a yang line at the top “Nine at the top.”\n\n## Proper Place and Centrality\nPositions one, three and five are odd, yang places; two, four and the top are even, yin places. A yang line in a yang place or a yin line in a yin place is “in its proper place,” also called correct; otherwise it is “out of place.” The second line sits in the middle of the lower trigram and the fifth in the middle of the upper, so both are called central. The Zhouyi prizes centrality, often even above being in the proper place. Nine in the fifth place is both central and correct, the most honored position in a hexagram; the Chinese expression “the honor of nine-five,” meaning the throne, comes from this. In After Completion all six lines are in their proper places; in Before Completion none are. One signifies things done, the other things not yet done.\n\n## Resonance and Adjacency\nThe relation between corresponding positions in the lower and upper trigrams (first and fourth, second and fifth, third and top) is called resonance. When one is yin and the other yang, they resonate and can respond to and support each other; two yin or two yang lines do not. The relation between neighboring lines, such as second and third or fourth and fifth, is called adjacency; how close neighbors are, and which supports or rides above the other, also shapes a line's situation.\n\n## The Character of Each Place\nThe Great Commentary, Part II, sums it up: “The second and the fourth share a function but differ in position, and their good differs: the second has much praise, the fourth much fear, because it is near… The third and the fifth share a function but differ in position: the third has much misfortune, the fifth much merit, because of the difference between noble and humble.” The second line is central and far from the ruler's seat, so it often earns praise; the fourth sits right next to the fifth, in a sensitive spot, and is often anxious; the third is at the top of the lower trigram, caught between advancing and retreating, and often in danger; the fifth is central and honored and can often achieve things. Once you understand the positions, you can see why the line texts of a single hexagram sometimes promise good fortune and sometimes warn of misfortune.\n\n## Time and Place\nTo read a line, look at both its time and its place. The Creative is all yang lines, yet in the first place it is “Hidden dragon. Do not act,” in the fifth “Flying dragon in the heavens,” and at the top “Arrogant dragon will have cause to regret.” A different position means a different situation, and a different way to respond.";

"kb.change.title" = "Changing Lines & Hexagrams";
"kb.change.sub" = "Old lines change, young stay: one becomes two";
"kb.change.body" = "## Old and Young\nWhen you cast, each line comes out as one of the numbers six, seven, eight or nine. Seven is Young Yang and eight is Young Yin: their strength is still growing and they stay in place, so they are unchanging lines. Nine is Old Yang and six is Old Yin: they have reached their peak and must transform, so they are changing lines. Yang at its extreme gives rise to yin, and yin at its extreme gives rise to yang, so Old Yang turns into a yin line and Old Yin into a yang line: this is the principle that “at the limit, things change.” With coins, each line has a one-in-four chance of changing, about one and a half changing lines per hexagram on average. One or two changing lines are most common, no changing lines happens nearly one time in five, and all six changing is extremely rare.\n\n## Primary and Resulting Hexagrams\nDrawing the six lines as they came gives the primary hexagram, which shows your present situation. Flipping every changing line to its opposite gives a new hexagram, the resulting hexagram, which hints at where things may go. If all six lines are sevens and eights, there are no changing lines and so no resulting hexagram, and the app shows “No changing lines, so no resulting hexagram.”\n\n## The Coin Method\nThis app's coin method follows the traditional coin oracle: toss three coins together; the inscribed side counts 2 and the plain side 3, and the three are added up:\nThree inscribed sides make 6: Old Yin, a yin line, changing;\ntwo inscribed and one plain make 7: Young Yang, a yang line, unchanging;\none inscribed and two plain make 8: Young Yin, a yin line, unchanging;\nthree plain sides make 9: Old Yang, a yang line, changing.\nToss six times in all: the first toss gives Line 1 and the sixth gives Line 6, building the hexagram from the bottom up. In the app, Old Yang is marked “○” and Old Yin “×,” both drawn in the accent color.\n\n## An Example\nSuppose six tosses give, from the bottom up, 7, 8, 9, 7, 8, 8. The lower trigram is Fire and the upper is Thunder, so the primary hexagram is Abundance (Feng). The third line is a 9, Old Yang; once it becomes a yin line, both trigrams are Thunder, and the resulting hexagram is The Arousing (Zhen). With only one changing line, the rules say to read the line text of Nine in the third place of Abundance.\n\n## Reading the Two Hexagrams\nThe primary and resulting hexagrams are like the present and future of a matter, its starting point and its destination. When reading, start with the primary hexagram to understand where you stand; then look at the changing lines to see where change begins; finally turn to the resulting hexagram to sense where things are heading. The changing lines are the bridge between the two hexagrams, and the part most worth pondering.";

"kb.methods.title" = "Ways to Cast";
"kb.methods.sub" = "Yarrow stalks, coins and Plum Blossom numerology";
"kb.methods.body" = "## The Yarrow-Stalk Method\nThe oldest method appears in the Great Commentary, Part I: “The number of the great expansion is fifty, of which forty-nine are used.” Take fifty yarrow stalks and set one aside. Divide the remaining forty-nine at random into two piles, representing heaven and earth; take one stalk from one pile and hold it between your fingers, representing humanity; then count off each pile in groups of four and set the remainders aside. Doing this three times is called the three changes; three changes produce one line, and eighteen produce a hexagram. After each round of three changes, the number of stalks left, divided by four, can only be six, seven, eight or nine, which is where the line numbers come from. The method is solemn and slow, and the ancients used it to show how seriously they took the question.\n\n## The Coin Oracle\nFrom the Tang and Song dynasties on, people commonly used three coins instead of yarrow stalks, a practice traditionally called the coin oracle and associated with the book Huozhulin. One toss of three coins gives one line and six tosses make a hexagram. It is simple and convenient and yields the same old and young yin and yang as the yarrow method, though each kind of line comes up with slightly different odds. This app's Coin toss uses this method.\n\n## Plum Blossom Numerology\nThe Plum Blossom Numerology (Meihua Yishu), traditionally attributed to Shao Yong of the Northern Song, casts hexagrams from numbers drawn from whatever moment or thing is at hand. This app uses two of its methods, both taking trigrams by the Early Heaven numbers (Heaven 1, Lake 2, Fire 3, Thunder 4, Wind 5, Water 6, Mountain 7, Earth 8).\n\nNumber cast: write down three positive whole numbers by intuition. The remainder of the first number divided by 8 sets the upper trigram, and the remainder of the second divided by 8 sets the lower trigram; a remainder of 0 counts as 8, Earth. The remainder of the third number divided by 6 sets the changing line; a remainder of 0 counts as 6, the top line. For example, take 15, 22 and 9: 15 leaves 7, Mountain; 22 leaves 6, Water; 9 leaves 3. That gives Mountain over Water, Youthful Folly (Meng), with the third line changing. The original book usually derives the changing line from the sum of the numbers; this app lets the third number decide it on its own, which is easier to follow.\n\nTime cast: the year is counted by its Earthly Branch, from 1 for a Zi year and 2 for a Chou year up to 12 for a Hai year; add the lunar month and the lunar day, and the remainder of this sum divided by 8 sets the upper trigram. Add the branch number of the two-hour period (1 for the Zi hour up to 12 for the Hai hour) to that sum; the remainder divided by 8 sets the lower trigram, and the remainder of this total divided by 6 sets the changing line. A remainder of 0 again counts as 8 or 6. This app converts to the lunar calendar using Beijing time and counts a leap month as its regular month; after 11 p.m. it is already the Zi hour, and the date counts as the next day. Every cast within the same two-hour period gives the same hexagram, in keeping with the principle of not divining twice on one matter.";

"kb.rules.title" = "Reading a Cast";
"kb.rules.sub" = "Zhu Xi's rules, and the spirit of divination";
"kb.rules.body" = "## Which Text to Read\nOnce the hexagram is cast, the primary and resulting hexagrams together offer two Judgments and twelve line texts. Which should take the lead? Opinions have long differed. This app follows the rules for changing lines set out by the Southern Song scholar Zhu Xi in his Introduction to the Study of the Yi (Yixue Qimeng), decided by the number of changing lines:\nNo changing lines: read the Judgment of the primary hexagram;\none changing line: read that line's text in the primary hexagram;\ntwo changing lines: read both line texts in the primary hexagram, with the upper one leading;\nthree changing lines: read the Judgments of the primary and resulting hexagrams, with the primary leading;\nfour changing lines: read the texts of the two unchanging lines in the resulting hexagram, with the lower one leading;\nfive changing lines: read the text of the one unchanging line in the resulting hexagram;\nall six lines change: for The Creative, read “All Nines”: “A flight of dragons without heads appears. Good fortune.” For The Receptive, read “All Sixes”: “Lasting perseverance brings benefit.” For any other hexagram, read the Judgment of the resulting hexagram.\n\n## The Thinking Behind the Rules\nWith few changing lines, change is concentrated in one or two places, so the focus is on the changing lines of the primary hexagram. With many, the matter has largely turned, so the weight shifts to the resulting hexagram and the parts of it that have not changed. Zhu Xi's original makes finer distinctions for three changing lines; this app simplifies them and lets the primary hexagram lead.\n\n## The Spirit of Divination\nThe ancients said, “Without doubt, do not divine”: if your mind is already made up, or common sense can settle the matter, there is no need to consult the oracle. They also said, “one matter, one reading”: ask about one specific matter at a time, and do not ask the same question again and again. As the Judgment of Youthful Folly says, “At the first divination I answer. Asking two or three times is importunity, and to the importunate I give no answer.”\n\nMore important still, the Judgments and line texts are not verdicts. The Zhouyi often says “perseverance brings good fortune,” “no blame” or “remorse vanishes”: good or bad fortune often depends on how you respond. The Great Commentary says, “‘No blame’ means being good at mending one's faults”: correct your mistakes in time, and no harm follows. Think of the hexagram as a mirror. It shows where you stand now, what you may be overlooking, and how far to advance or hold back, but the final choice always remains in your hands. Xunzi said, “Those who are good at the Changes do not divine”: once you truly grasp the principles of change, you can see the way forward for yourself.";

"kb.tenwings.title" = "The Ten Wings";
"kb.tenwings.sub" = "Ten commentaries that lend the classic wings";
"kb.tenwings.body" = "## What Are the Ten Wings?\nA “wing” here means a support, like the wings of a bird. The ten commentaries that explain the classic are called the Ten Wings; since the Han dynasty they have circulated together with the classic and are known collectively as the Commentaries (Yizhuan). The received edition splits the Tuan and Xiang commentaries among the hexagrams and appends the Wenyan to The Creative and The Receptive, so classic and commentary alternate as you read:\nCommentary on the Judgments (Tuan), Parts I and II: explains each hexagram's name and Judgment and its overall meaning;\nCommentary on the Images (Xiang), Parts I and II: the Great Images draw a lesson in self-cultivation for the noble person from the images of the two trigrams, such as “Heaven moves with vigor; thus the noble person strengthens themselves without ceasing,” while the Small Images explain each line text;\nGreat Commentary (Xici), Parts I and II: discusses the principles of the Changes and the method of divination, the most philosophical part of the commentaries;\nCommentary on the Words of the Text (Wenyan): an in-depth reading of The Creative and The Receptive;\nDiscussion of the Trigrams (Shuogua): on the directions and images of the eight trigrams;\nSequence of the Hexagrams (Xugua): explains the order of the sixty-four hexagrams;\nMiscellaneous Notes on the Hexagrams (Zagua): sets the order aside and pairs hexagrams in contrast to sum up each one's essence.\n\n## Authorship and the Worn-Out Bindings\nTradition credits the Ten Wings to Confucius. The Records of the Grand Historian, in “The Hereditary House of Confucius,” says that in his later years Confucius loved the Changes: “He read the Changes until the leather cords binding the bamboo slips broke three times.” As early as the Song dynasty, Ouyang Xiu doubted that the Ten Wings came from a single hand; most scholars today believe they were written by Confucian scholars between the Warring States period and the Qin and Han, though many of their ideas follow in Confucius's line of thought.\n\n## Famous Lines from the Great Commentary\n“One yin, one yang: this is called the Way.” The world runs on the alternation and cooperation of two forces.\n“Ceaseless generation is what is called change.” Life giving rise to life without end: this is the root meaning of Yi.\n“At the limit, things change; by changing, they flow through; by flowing through, they endure.” At a dead end, transform; transformation opens the way, and an open way lasts.\n“Writing cannot fully express speech, and speech cannot fully express meaning.” Words have limits, so the sages “set up images to express meaning fully,” which is why the Zhouyi speaks through the images of its hexagrams.\n“Like gathers with like, and things divide into groups.” This saying, still a common idiom in Chinese today, also comes from the Great Commentary.\n“At rest, the noble person contemplates the images and savors the words; in action, contemplates the changes and savors the divination.” Read the images and texts closely day to day, and look to change and divination when a decision arrives. This is also how this column hopes you will use the Zhouyi: as a way to reflect on yourself, not something to hand your fate over to.";

"kb.dayan.title" = "Casting with Yarrow Stalks";
"kb.dayan.sub" = "Four operations, eighteen changes, and the odds of old and young";
"kb.dayan.body" = "## What the Classic Records\nThe Great Commentary, Part I, preserves the oldest casting procedure we have: “The number of the great expansion is fifty, of which forty-nine are used. They are divided in two to represent the two; one is hung up to represent the three; they are counted off by fours to represent the four seasons; the remainder is set aside between the fingers to represent the intercalary month. In five years there are two intercalary months, so the remainder is set aside twice before hanging up again.” It adds that “four operations complete a change, and eighteen changes complete a hexagram.” Dividing, hanging one, counting by fours and setting aside the remainder are the four operations, and together they make one change. Each step mirrors heaven and earth, humanity, the seasons and the calendar, so the act of casting was seen as a small model of the way the cosmos moves.\n\n## Three Changes Make a Line\nFirst change: divide the forty-nine stalks, hang one, count off by fours, and the two remainders plus the hung stalk always total either five or nine; set them aside. The second change repeats the same steps with the stalks that are left, and this time either four or eight are set aside; the third change is the same. After three changes, the stalks remaining can only be thirty-six, thirty-two, twenty-eight or twenty-four. Divided by four, they give nine, eight, seven or six: Old Yang, Young Yin, Young Yang or Old Yin. That is one line, and six lines take eighteen changes. The Southern Song scholar Zhu Xi set out every step, from laying out the table and lighting incense to holding and dividing the stalks, in his Rite of Divination (Shiyi), printed at the head of his Original Meaning of the Zhouyi (Zhouyi Benyi), and later practice has largely followed it.\n\n## How It Differs from Coins\nWorked out exactly, each line has a 1 in 16 chance of being six, 5 in 16 of being seven, 7 in 16 of being eight and 3 in 16 of being nine. With coins the odds are 1/8, 3/8, 3/8 and 1/8. Side by side, the chance of a changing line is the same, one in four, and yin and yang lines are equally likely in both. The difference lies among the changing lines: with yarrow, Old Yang and Old Yin come up three to one, while with coins they come up one to one. In other words, yarrow lets yang lines change more often and yin lines less often, and Old Yin is the rarest outcome of all.\n\n## How This App Does It\nCasting with fifty real stalks takes a quarter of an hour or so per hexagram. This app's yarrow method does not simulate the dividing of stalks; it draws each line directly with the four exact probabilities above, so the results follow the same distribution as stalks counted by hand. If you want something of the ancients' care, sit quietly for a moment before you begin and get clear on what you are asking.";

"kb.tiyong.title" = "Body and Function in Plum Blossom";
"kb.tiyong.sub" = "The body is you; the function is the matter at hand";
"kb.tiyong.body" = "## Which Is Body, Which Is Function\nBody and function (ti and yong) are the heart of reading in Plum Blossom Numerology (Meihua Yishu). A hexagram is made of an upper and a lower trigram. The trigram containing the changing line is the function, standing for the matter you ask about and the people and circumstances around it; the trigram without it is the body, standing for you, the one who asks. If the changing line is the first, second or third, the lower trigram is the function and the upper the body; if it is the fourth, fifth or top line, the upper trigram is the function and the lower the body. Number and time casts always have exactly one changing line, so body and function are clear. When a coin or yarrow cast has no changing line, or more than one, they cannot be told apart cleanly, and this app does not show them.\n\n## The Five Phases\nBody and function are compared through the Five Phases. Each trigram belongs to one: Heaven and Lake are Metal, Fire is Fire, Thunder and Wind are Wood, Water is Water, Mountain and Earth are Earth. The phases generate one another in the order Wood, Fire, Earth, Metal, Water and back to Wood; each one restrains the phase two steps ahead: Wood restrains Earth, Earth restrains Water, Water restrains Fire, Fire restrains Metal, Metal restrains Wood. That leaves only five possible relations between them: harmony, when both share a phase; function nourishes body; body nourishes function; function restrains body; and body restrains function.\n\n## The Traditional Reading\nBroadly, the book treats harmony, function nourishing body and body restraining function as favorable, and body nourishing function or function restraining body as unfavorable: when function nourishes body there is gain, when body nourishes function there is drain, and when function restrains body things meet obstruction. It then weighs further factors, such as whether the body trigram is strong in the current season and what omens accompany the cast. This is the accumulated lore of earlier readers. This app marks only the relation itself, with no verdict and no score: what you gain and what you give are often two sides of one thing, and the meaning of a relation only shows when you set it against your own situation.\n\n## Beginning, Middle and End\nBody and function are not read in the primary hexagram alone. The nuclear hexagram, built from lines two to four as the lower trigram and lines three to five as the upper, stands for how things unfold along the way; the resulting hexagram, obtained once the changing line turns, stands for where they arrive. Primary is the beginning, nuclear the middle, resulting the end. The body trigram does not change in the resulting hexagram, so reading the three stages together shows how the phase facing the body shifts step by step, and whether the relation grows easier or tighter. This app lists the function of the primary hexagram, the nuclear hexagram and the function of the resulting hexagram in turn, for you to reflect on.";

"kb.najia.title" = "Six Lines and Najia";
"kb.najia.sub" = "Jing Fang's eight palaces and the elements of a chart";
"kb.najia.body" = "## Eight Palaces, Self and Other\nSix-line najia divination goes back to Jing Fang of the Western Han, and later came to be called the Huozhulin method. Jing Fang's Commentary on the Changes (Jingshi Yizhuan) divides the sixty-four hexagrams among eight palaces: Heaven, Thunder, Water, Mountain, Earth, Wind, Fire and Lake. Each palace is headed by its doubled trigram, the palace head. Changing its lines one at a time from the bottom gives the first- through fifth-generation hexagrams; changing the fourth line of the fifth-generation hexagram gives the wandering soul; restoring the inner trigram of the wandering soul to the palace trigram gives the returning soul. Every hexagram has a self line (shi) standing for the one who asks: the top line in the palace head, the line of the same number in the first- to fifth-generation hexagrams, the fourth line in a wandering soul and the third in a returning soul. The line three places from the self is the other (ying), standing for the other party or the matter asked about. A hexagram takes the phase of its palace: Coming to Meet (Gou), for example, belongs to the Heaven palace and is Metal.\n\n## Stems, Branches and the Six Relations\nNajia, literally “receiving the stems,” assigns a Heavenly Stem and an Earthly Branch to each line. Heaven takes Jia in the inner trigram and Ren in the outer, Earth takes Yi inside and Gui outside, Thunder takes Geng, Wind Xin, Water Wu, Fire Ji, Mountain Bing and Lake Ding. Branches run forward for yang trigrams and backward for yin ones; Heaven, from the bottom up, reads Zi, Yin, Chen, Wu, Shen, Xu. Each branch has a phase, and taking the palace phase as the self gives the six relations: the same phase is Siblings, what the self generates is Offspring, what it restrains is Wealth, what restrains it is Officials, and what generates it is Parents. Jing Fang's text already holds the germ of this scheme; the names were fixed later.\n\n## Six Spirits, Void Branches, Hidden Spirits\nThe six spirits are laid from the first line upward according to the day's stem: Jia and Yi days start with the Azure Dragon, Bing and Ding with the Vermilion Bird, Wu with the Gouchen, Ji with the Soaring Serpent (Tengshe), Geng and Xin with the White Tiger, Ren and Gui with the Black Tortoise, cycling in that order. Void branches follow the ten-day period of the day pillar: ten stems meet twelve branches, so each period leaves two branches empty; in the Jia-Zi period, Xu and Hai are void. If one of the six relations is missing from the hexagram, it is sought in the palace head; the line found there is the hidden spirit, lying beneath the flying spirit at the same position.\n\n## Month and Day\nReading the lines also involves the month branch and the day. The month changes at the solar terms, so the Yin month begins only at the Beginning of Spring; the day is that day's stem and branch. A line supported by the month or day is strong, one restrained by them weak, and these are then weighed together with changing lines and void branches. Qing manuals such as Additions and Deletions to Divination (Zengshan Buyi) and The True Lineage of Divination (Bushi Zhengzong) discuss this at length, and schools differ in the details.\n\n## How This App Does It\nThis app only lays out the chart: palace, self and other, stems and branches, the six relations, the six spirits, hidden spirits and changed lines, with the stems and branches of the year, month, day and hour and the void branches. It does not pick a focal line, judge strength or reach a conclusion. The layout is objective; the reading belongs to you, so treat the chart as a map for careful looking.";
